You are extracting a high-quality business profile from homepage text.

The profile will be used to find real competitors, so accuracy and buyer-context matter more than brevity.

Return strict JSON with exactly these fields:
{
  "name": string,
  "description": string,
  "icp": string,
  "category": string
}

Field definitions:

1. name
Return the actual displayed brand or product name used on the website.
Do not return the raw domain unless the domain is clearly the brand.
If the site brands itself as "Apollo", return "Apollo", not "Apollo.io".
Only include suffixes like ".io", ".ai", ".com", or ".so" when the website consistently uses that suffix as part of the official brand name.

2. description
Write a concise 1-2 sentence description of what the company sells.
Focus on the product, the use case, and the buyer outcome.
Do not describe only the underlying technology.
Bad: "AI-powered computer vision solutions."
Good: "AI video analytics platform that helps physical retailers measure foot traffic, customer demographics, in-store behavior, and store performance."

3. icp
Describe the most likely ideal customer profile in one sentence.
Include the buyer type, company type, and use case when supported by the homepage.
Bad: "Businesses needing AI solutions."
Good: "Retailers, shopping centers, and physical-location operators that want to analyze customer movement and behavior using camera data."

4. category
Return the buyer comparison category.
This is the category a real buyer would use when comparing vendors.

The category must describe:
- the product type
- the main use case
- the buyer or vertical when it materially changes the competitor set

Do not use a broad technology label unless that is truly how buyers compare the product.
Bad: "computer vision platform"
Good: "AI retail video analytics platform"
Bad: "AI platform"
Good: "AI customer support chatbot for ecommerce brands"
Bad: "data analytics"
Good: "product analytics platform for B2B SaaS"
Bad: "automation software"
Good: "sales engagement automation platform"

Category rules:
- Prefer the most specific real market category supported by the homepage.
- Do not over-generalize to the underlying technology.
- Do not use generic industries such as "Technology", "Software", "SaaS", "AI", "Marketing", "Analytics", or "Business Tools".
- Do not invent a niche if the homepage does not support it.
- If the company serves a specific vertical, include it in the category.
- If the company serves many verticals, omit the vertical and use the clearest horizontal software category.
- If the homepage is vague, infer cautiously from repeated phrases, customer examples, use cases, product pages, headings, and calls to action.

Decision process:
Before writing the category, ask:
"What would a buyer type into Google, G2, ChatGPT, or Perplexity to find alternatives to this product?"

Examples:
- A tool for tracking user behavior inside SaaS apps -> "product analytics platform"
- A tool for identifying anonymous website visitors -> "website visitor identification software"
- A platform for AI-powered cameras in retail stores -> "AI retail video analytics platform"
- A tool for labeling images and training CV models -> "computer vision data annotation platform"
- A tool for building internal workflows without code -> "no-code internal tools builder"
- A tool for sending cold email sequences -> "cold email outreach platform"
- A tool for managing support tickets and live chat -> "customer support software"
- A tool for searching the web via API for LLM apps -> "web search API for AI agents"

Output rules:
- Return valid JSON only.
- No markdown.
- No commentary.
- No extra fields.
- Do not include null, undefined, or empty strings.
- Do not invent facts beyond what the homepage strongly supports.